<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Manley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Manley was Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> from 1972 to 1980 and from 1989 to 1992, and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-party">People's National Party</a> founded by his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-manley">Norman Manley</a>. In his first term he pursued democratic socialism, raised the levy on bauxite and drew close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, amid economic crisis and political violence. In his second term he adopted market-oriented policies.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michael Manley was the second son of Norman Washington Manley, later premier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, and the artist Edna Manley. He studied at the London School of Economics from 1945, where Fabian socialism and the writings of Harold Laski influenced him. After graduating in 1949 he edited and wrote for the newspaper Public Opinion and became a negotiator for the National Workers Union. He entered the Senate in 1962 and the House of Representatives in 1967, and in 1969, after his father retired, he was elected leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-party">People's National Party</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The party won the 1972 general election in a landslide, taking 37 of 53 seats from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica-labour-party">Jamaica Labour Party</a> of Hugh Shearer. Manley's government introduced a minimum wage covering domestic workers, equal pay for women, maternity leave, wider free education and the National Housing Trust, under which more than 40,000 houses were built between 1974 and 1980. Abroad he befriended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> and Olof Palme, and his support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>'s sending of troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, criticised by Henry Kissinger and others, strained relations with the US.</p><p>Political violence between supporters of the two parties reached levels not seen before. After a wave of killings in 1974 his government passed the Gun Court Act and the Suppression of Crime Act, and a state of emergency declared in June 1976 saw about 500 people detained without charge. The party won the December 1976 election with 47 seats to 13, but in 1980, an election year in which over 800 Jamaicans were killed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-seaga">Edward Seaga</a>'s Jamaica Labour Party won 51 of 60 seats.</p><p>Manley led the opposition through the 1980s and returned as prime minister after the 1989 election, in which the party won 45 seats to 15. His second term focused on liberalising the economy, with a Social Support Programme to cushion the effects of austerity. In 1992, citing health reasons, he stepped down as prime minister and party leader, and P. J. Patterson succeeded him in both offices.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Manley wrote seven books, including A History of West Indies Cricket, which discussed the links between cricket and West Indian nationalism. He died of prostate cancer on 6 March 1997 and was given a state funeral on 16 March, attended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a> among others. He is interred at National Heroes Park, where his father is also buried.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael Manley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Michael Manley's</b> democratic socialist government of 1972 to 1980 raised the bauxite levy and allied with Cuba, while political violence culminated in about 800 deaths around the 1980 election, won by Edward Seaga.</blockquote><a href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingston-jamaica">Kingston</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.97,-76.79&amp;year=1971" aria-label="Kingston on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2467 1497 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Michael Manley"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2917 1767h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2917 1767h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=17.97,-76.79&amp;year=1971">What was here in 1971? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
